WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460 line:84%
JUSTIN JACKSON: You're not
going to get psychological depth

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.760 line:84%
anywhere else in the
Hebrew Bible the way

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.680 line:84%
in which you get it
here in the David story.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.240 line:84%
The David story confronts
us with those huge questions

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.500 line:90%
of life itself.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.930 line:84%
What is man's
relationship to God?

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.580 line:84%
What is man's relationship
to his fellow man?

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.950 line:84%
What is a parent's
relationship to their children?

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.870 line:84%
We understand all
of this when we

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:30.170 line:84%
see David there with
the death of his child

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.860 line:84%
and knowing he's fully
responsible for all of this.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.800 line:84%
It is the horror of each
one of his children.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.100 line:84%
David knows that it
always goes back to him.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.190 line:84%
Human beings will not always
do what we ought to do.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.810 line:90%
The solution is simple.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.100 line:90%
One repents.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.070 line:84%
I think this is what David
has learned, and, my goodness,

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:51.450 line:90%
that's not a failure.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.970 line:84%
I would say that's precisely
what makes David a hero.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.000 line:84%
I would be delighted if you
would join me as we go through

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.690 line:84%
and look at the David story
here to see the tragedy of Saul,

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.450 line:84%
see how confused
a man that he is,

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.630 line:84%
see both how obedient and
simultaneously disobedient he

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:12.700 line:84%
is, to join me as we look
at David, a man who misuses

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.020 line:84%
his kingship and then somehow,
even in the face of disaster,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.310 line:90%
he finds his way out of it.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.310 line:84%
He repents, and he
turns back to God.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.835 line:84%
And we'll be on that
floor with David

00:01:24.835 --> 00:01:28.140 line:84%
as he's weeping tears for a
child that's going to die.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.140 line:84%
And then in the stunning
moment when he finds out

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.250 line:84%
that he's dead, he gets up,
and he can then be able to say,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.560 line:90%
he's not coming back to me.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.890 line:90%
Rather, I am going to him.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.150 line:90%
What does this mean?

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:47.950 line:84%
And in what ways does this mark
out the rest of the disasters

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.230 line:90%
of David's life?

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.720 line:84%
What it will offer
to us is not a sense

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.540 line:90%
of who David was historically.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.630 line:84%
I think it's supposed
to be asking us,

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.250 line:90%
what are you as a human person?

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:06.030 line:84%
I'm Justin Jackson, professor
of English at Hillsdale College.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:10.160 line:84%
This is our course
on the David story.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.910 line:90%
Let's begin.

